          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            What is commonly called
            %em Noether’s theorem
            or
            %em Noether’s first theorem
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorem"} theorem
            due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emmy+Noether"} Emmy Noether
            (
            %a{:href => "#Noether1918"}> Noether 1918
            ) which makes precise and asserts that to every continuous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+of+the+Lagrangian"} symmetry of the Lagrangian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"}> prequantum field theory
            ) there is naturally associated a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservation+law"} conservation law
            stating the conservation of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge"} charge
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conserved+current"}> conserved current
            ) when the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            hold.
          %p
            For instance the
            = succeed "-translation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"} time
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            equivalently means that the quantity of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            is conserved, and the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/translation+group"} translation
            invariant of a physical system means that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momentum"} momentum
            is preserved.
          %p
            The original and still most common formulation of the theorem is in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational calculus
            applied to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local action functional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+bicomplex"} variational bicomplex
            we discuss below in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InTermsOfTheVariationalBicomplex"} Lagrangian version – In terms of the variational bicomplex
          %p
            There is another formulation of the same physical content, but using the formalism of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            the relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetries+of+the+Lagrangian"} symmetries of the Lagrangian
            and
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charges"} charges
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conserved+currents"} conserved currents
            happens to be built deep into the formalism in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+flows"} Hamiltonian flows
            generated by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            function. Accordingly, in this powerful formalism Noether’s theorem becomes almost a tautology. This we discuss in
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#HamiltonianNoetherTheorem"} Hamiltonian/symplectic version – In terms of moment maps
              \.
          %h2#InTermsOfTheVariationalBicomplex Lagrangian version
          %p
            Here we formulate Noether’s theorem for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local action functional
            in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+bicomplex"} variational bicomplex
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
          %h3#LagrangianVersionSimpleSchematicIdea Simple schematic idea
          %p Before coming to the precise and general formulation, we indicate here schematically the simple idea which underlies Noether’s first theorem (in its original Lagrangian version).
          %p
            Consider a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"} local Lagrangian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and assume for simplicity that it depends only on the first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla \phi
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gradient"} gradient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L \colon \phi \mapsto L(\phi, \nabla \phi)
                  \\,.
          %p
            (We write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla \cdot (-)
            in the following for the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergence"} divergence
          %p
            Then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+derivative"} variational derivative
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            by the fields is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi δ
                          %mi L
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %mfrac
                              %mi δ
                              %mrow
                                %mi δ
                                %mi ϕ
                            %mi L
                            %mo )
                          %mi δ
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo +
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %mfrac
                              %mi δ
                              %mrow
                                %mi δ
                                %mo ∇
                                %mi ϕ
                            %mi L
                            %mo )
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mo ∇
                          %mi δ
                          %mi ϕ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %mfrac
                              %mi δ
                              %mrow
                                %mi δ
                                %mi ϕ
                            %mi L
                            %mo −
                            %mo ∇
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mfrac
                                %mi δ
                                %mrow
                                  %mi δ
                                  %mo ∇
                                  %mi ϕ
                              %mi L
                              %mo )
                            %mo )
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi δ
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo +
                          %mo ∇
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mfrac
                                %mi δ
                                %mrow
                                  %mi δ
                                  %mo ∇
                                  %mi ϕ
                              %mi L
                              %mo )
                            %mi δ
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\delta L
                  \&amp; =
                  \\left(\frac{\delta}{\delta \phi} L\right)  \delta \phi
                  +
                  \\left(\frac{\delta}{\delta \nabla \phi} L\right) \cdot \nabla \delta \phi
                  \\\
                  \&amp; =
                  \\left(
                  \\frac{\delta}{\delta \phi} L
                  \-
                  \\nabla \cdot \left(\frac{\delta}{\delta \nabla\phi}L\right)
                  \\right)
                  \\cdot (\delta \phi)
                  +
                  \\nabla \cdot \left( \left(\frac{\delta }{\delta \nabla \phi} L\right)
                  \\delta \phi
                  \\right)
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where in the second step the total derivative was introduced via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+rule"} product rule
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi g
                    %mo +
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f (\nabla g) = -(\nabla f) g + \nabla (f g)
          %p
            From this law for the variation of the Lagrangian, one derives both the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            as well as Noether’s theorem by making different assumptions and setting different terms to zero:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Demanding that the variation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \phi
                vanishes on some
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                implies that the rightmost term in the above equation disappears in the variation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mi S
                      %mo =
                      %mi δ
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta S = \delta \int L
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
                (by the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes+theorem"} Stokes theorem
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mi S
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta S = 0
                under variation that vanishes on the boundary is equivalent to demanding the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mi δ
                        %mrow
                          %mi δ
                          %mi ϕ
                      %mi L
                      %mo −
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mfrac
                          %mi δ
                          %mrow
                            %mi δ
                            %mo ∇
                            %mi ϕ
                        %mi L
                        %mo )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\frac{\delta}{\delta \phi} L
                      \-
                      \\nabla \cdot \left(\frac{\delta}{\delta \nabla\phi}L\right)
                      \= 0
                      \\,.
            %li
              %p
                On the other hand, assuming that for given
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \phi
                the variation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta L
                vanishes when these equations of motion hold – hence assuming that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \phi
                is an
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+of+the+Lagrangian"} symmetry
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                – is equivalent to assuming that the above expression is zero even without the left term, hence that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mfrac
                        %mi δ
                        %mrow
                          %mi δ
                          %mo ∇
                          %mi ϕ
                      %mi L
                      %mo )
                    %mi δ
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nabla \cdot
                  \\left(\left(\frac{\delta }{\delta \nabla\phi} L\right) \delta \phi\right)
                  \= 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the statement of
            = succeed "." do
              %em Noether’s theorem
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi ϕ
                  %mi δ
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac
                      %mi δ
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %mo ∇
                        %mi ϕ
                    %mi L
                    %mo )
                  %mi δ
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  p_\phi \delta \phi
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\left(\frac{\delta }{\delta \nabla \phi} L\right) \delta \phi
          %p
            (here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\phi
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+momentum"} canonical momentum
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noether+current"} Noether current
            and the above says that this is (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"}> on-shell
            ) a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conserved+current"} conserved current
            precisely if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \phi
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+of+the+Lagrangian"} symmetry of the Lagrangian
          %p
            This is at least the way that Noether’s theorem has been introduced and is often considered. But this formulation is more restrictive than is natural. Namely it is unnatural to demand of a symmetry that it leaves the Lagrangian entirely invariant,
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta L = 0
          %p#WeakSymmetrySchematicIdea
            More generally for the symmetry to be a symmetry of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int L
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
            it is sufficient that the Lagrangian changes by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergence"} divergence
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta L = \nabla \cdot \sigma
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            (This is really a sign of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+symmetry"} higher gauge symmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wess-Zumino-Witten+model"} Wess-Zumino-Witten model
            because the WZW term is not strictly invariant under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "conserved+current#InHigherPrequantumGeometry"} conserved current – In higher prequantum geometry
          %p
            In this more general case the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservation+law"} conservation law
            induced by the “weak” symmetry becomes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi ϕ
                    %mi δ
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo −
                    %mi σ
                    %mo )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nabla \cdot
                  \\left(
                  p_\phi \delta \phi - \sigma
                  \\right)
                  \= 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            (This may be regarded as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Legendre+transform"} Legendre transform
            of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %h3#LagrangianVersionFormalContext Formulation via the variational bicomplex
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to X
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Jet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Jet(E) \to X
            its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
            and write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo ,
                      %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Jet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi H
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^{\bullet,\bullet}(Jet(E)), (d = d_H + d_V)
          %p
            the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+bicomplex"} variational bicomplex
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_V
            being the vertical and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_H
            the horizontal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
          #VariationOfTheLagrangian.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Jet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{L} \in \Omega^{n,0}(Jet(E))
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"} local Lagrangian
              we have a unique decomposition of its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi V
                    %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi H
                    %mi Θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d L = d_V L = \mathbf{E} - d_H \Theta
            %p
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source+form"} source form
              – the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+form"} Euler-Lagrange form
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{L}
              – and for some
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Θ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi j
                        %mn ∞
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta \in \Omega^{n-1,0}(j_\infty E)
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The
              %em
                dynamical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shell"} shell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Jet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E} \hookrightarrow Jet(E)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+locus"} zero locus
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
              together with its differential consequences.
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
              of the Lagrangian is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+locus"} zero locus
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\{\phi \in \Gamma(E) | \mathbf{E}(j_\infty \phi) = 0\}
            %p
              that solves the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \subset X
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} submanifold
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mi θ
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi δ
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi Σ
                    %mi Θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\delta \theta \coloneqq \delta \int_\Sigma \Theta
            %p
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+structure"} presymplectic structure
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
          #Symmetry.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong
                infinitesimal variational
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+of+the+Lagrangian"} symmetry
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{L}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+vector+field"} vertical vector field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi v
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi H
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{L}_v \mathbf{L} = d_H \sigma_v
            %p
              (with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}_v
              denoting the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+derivative"} Lie derivative
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Jet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\sigma_v \in \Omega^{n-1,0}(Jet(E))
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              By
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan%27s+magic+formula"} Cartan's magic formula
              and since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              is assumed vertical while
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is horizontal, this is equivalent to
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mi v
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi V
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi H
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mi v
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota_{v} d_V \mathbf{L} = d_H \sigma_v
                    \\,.
          #ConservedCurrent.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conserved+current"} conserved current
              is an element
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Jet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    j \in \Omega^{n-1, 0}(Jet(E))
            %p which is horizontally closed on the dynamical shell
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi H
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi ℰ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (d_H\, j)|_{\mathcal{E}} = 0
                    \\,.
          %p
            With the above notions and notation,
            %strong Noether’s theorem
            states:
          #NoetherTheoremViaVariationalBicomplex.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in T_v(j_\infty E)
              an infinitesimal variational symmetry according to def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Symmetry"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mi v
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mi v
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mi v
                    %mi Θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    j_v \coloneqq \sigma_v - \iota_v \Theta
            %p
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conserved+current"} conserved current
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConservedCurrent"}
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              By prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#VariationOfTheLagrangian"}
              and def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Symmetry"}
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mi H
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi σ
                              %mi v
                            %mo −
                            %msub
                              %mi ι
                              %mi v
                            %mi Θ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mi ι
                              %mi v
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mi V
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi L
                            %mo +
                            %msub
                              %mi ι
                              %mi v
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mi H
                            %mi Θ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mi ι
                              %mi v
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi E
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    d_H (\sigma_v - \iota_v \Theta)
                    \&amp; =
                    \\iota_v d_V \mathbf{L} + \iota_v d_H \Theta
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \iota_v \mathbf{E}
                    \\end{aligned}
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              A symmetry of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lepage+form"} Lepage form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo ≔
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                    %mo +
                    %mi Θ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\rho \coloneqq \mathbf{L} + \Theta
                    \\,.
            %p
              would be defined with the full differential
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi V
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = d_V + d_H
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi v
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mi v
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi κ
                      %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{L}_v \rho = d (\sigma_v + \kappa_v)
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              is horizontal and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
              is vertical. Decomposing the result into horizontal and vertical components, then for vertical
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              this is equivalent to the pair of equations
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi ι
                                %mi v
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi E
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %msub
                                %mi d
                                %mi H
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi σ
                                %mi v
                              %mo −
                              %msub
                                %mi ι
                                %mi v
                              %mi Θ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi ι
                                %mi v
                              %mi ω
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %msub
                                %mi d
                                %mi H
                              %msub
                                %mi κ
                                %mi v
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi d
                                %mi V
                              %msub
                                %mi σ
                                %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left\{
                    \\array{
                    \\iota_v \mathbf{E} &amp; = d_H (\sigma_v - \iota_v \Theta)
                    \\\
                    \\iota_v \omega &amp; = d_H \kappa_v + d_V \sigma_v
                    }
                    \\right.
            %p
              The first one expresses the conserved current corresponding to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              as in theorem
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NoetherTheoremViaVariationalBicomplex"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              to be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian vector field
              with respect to the presymplectic current.
          %h2#HamiltonianNoetherTheorem Hamiltonian/symplectic version – In terms of moment maps
          %h3#in_traditional_symplectic_geometry In traditional symplectic geometry
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            the analog of Noether’s theorem is the statement that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moment+map"} moment map
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            which preserves a given time evolution is itself conserved by this time evolution.
          %p
            Souriau called this
            = succeed "," do
              %em the symplectic Noether theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %em Hamiltonian Noether theorem
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Butterfield06"} Butterfield 06
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔓𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰𝔬𝔫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to \mathfrak{Poisson}(X,\omega)
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \in C^\infty(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
          %p
            Then let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔓𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰𝔬𝔫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} \to \mathfrak{Poisson}(X,\omega)
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            with
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moment+map"} moment map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi \in C^\infty(X,\mathfrak{g}^\ast)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            if for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi \in \mathfrak{g}
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            between the two vanishes,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi ξ
                  %mi H
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi Φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta_\xi H \coloneqq  \{\Phi(\xi), H\} = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            In this situation now the statement of Noether’s theorem is that the generators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi(\xi)
            of the symmetry are preserved by the time evolution
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi t
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi ξ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{d}{d t} \Phi^\xi = 0
                  \\,.
          %p In this symplectic formulation this is immediate, because
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi t
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi ξ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi ξ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{d}{d t}\Phi^\xi = \{H,\Phi^\xi\} = - \{\Phi^\xi, H\} = 0
                  \\,,
          %p
            by the above assumtion that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is preserved. Hence the “Hamiltonian Noether theorem” is all captured already by the very notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            and the statement that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            is skew-symmetric (is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            bracket).
          %p
            Specifically, if one has a global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+coordinates"} canonical coordinates
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{q^i\}
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+momenta"} canonical momenta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p_i\}
            and if the symmetry action is on the canonical coordinates (on configuration space), then for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi ξ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_\xi
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+field"} vector field
            corresponding to the generator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ξ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi
            the moment map is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi ξ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi ξ
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Phi^\xi = p_i (v_\xi)^i
                  \\,.
          %p
            The term on the right is in the form in which the conserved quantity obtained from Noether’s theorem is traditionally written (using that, given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mover
                            %mi q
                            %mo ˙
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_i = \frac{\delta L}{\delta (\dot q^i)}
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#energymomentum_current Energy-momentum current
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy-momentum+tensor"}> energy-momentum tensor
              – see at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory"} geometry of physics – A first idea of quantum field theory
              %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#ScalarFieldEnergyMomentum"} this example
          %h3#dirac_current Dirac current
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+current"}> Dirac current
              – see at
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory"} geometry of physics – A first idea of quantum field theory
              %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#DiracCurrent"} this example
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservation+law"} conservation law
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conserved+current"} conserved current
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge"} charge
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moment+map"} moment map
          %h2#References References
          %p The original article is
          %ul
            %li#Noether1918
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emmy+Noether"}> Emmy Noether
              ,
              %em Invariante Variationsprobleme
              Nachrichten der Königlichen Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften zu Göttingen, Math. Phys. Kl, 235 (1918). (
              %a{:href => "http://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/dms/load/img/?PID=GDZPPN00250510X"}> gdz
              )
          %p Lecture notes include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em Chapter 7, Noether’s theorem
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www-physics.ucsd.edu/students/courses/fall2010/physics110a/LECTURES/CH07.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Máximo Bañados, Ignacio A. Reyes,
                %em A short review on Noether’s theorems, gauge symmetries and boundary terms, for students
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1601.03616"}> arXiv:1601.03616
                )
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+perturbative+quantum+field+theory#Symmetries"} A first idea of quantum field theory – Symmetries
                (
                %a{:href => "https://www.physicsforums.com/insights/newideaofquantumfieldtheory-symmetries/"}> PF Insights version
                )
          %p A comprehensive exposition of both the Lagrangian and the Hamiltonian version of the theorem is in
          %ul
            %li#Butterfield06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeremy+Butterfield"}> Jeremy Butterfield
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On symmetry and conserved quantities in classical mechanics
              = succeed "," do
                %em Physical Theory and its Interpretation
              %p
                The Western Ontario Series in Philosophy of Science Volume 72, 2006, pp 43-100(2006) (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/ButterfieldNoether.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p Monographs:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yvette+Kosmann-Schwarzbach"}> Yvette Kosmann-Schwarzbach
                ,
                %em Les théorèmes de Noether:
              %p
                invariance et lois de conservation au XXe siècle : avec une traduction de l’article original, “Invariante Variationsprobleme”
                %em
                  , Editions Ecole Polytechnique, 2004 (
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.cornell.edu/~templier/junior/The-Noether-theorems.pdf"}> pdf
                  )
            %li#VinogradovKrasilshchik99
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandre+Vinogradov"}> Alexandre Vinogradov
                , I. S. Krasilshchik (eds.)
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Symmetries and Conservation Laws for Differential Equations of Mathematical Physics
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "ftp://softbank.iust.ac.ir/MathBooks/V/Vinogradov%20-%20Symmetries%20and%20Conservation%20Laws%20for%20Differential%20equations%20of%20mathematical%20physics.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Sardanashvily16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gennadi+Sardanashvily"}> Gennadi Sardanashvily
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Noether’s Theorems: Applications in Mechanics and Field Theory
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2991/978-94-6239-171-0"} doi:10.2991/978-94-6239-171-0
          %p
            For 1-parameter groups of symmetries in classical mechanics, the formulation and the proof of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noether%27s+theorem"} Noether's theorem
            can be found in the monograph
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Arnold"}> Vladimir Arnold
              ,
              %em Mathematical methods of classical mechanics
          %p
            For more general case see for instance the books by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Olver"} Peter Olver
          %p The Hamiltonian Noether theorem is also reviewed in a broader context of mathematical physics as theorem 7.3.2 in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+Paugam"}> Frédéric Paugam
              ,
              %em Towards the mathematics of quantum field theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.jussieu.fr/~fpaugam/documents/Towards-the-maths-of-QFT.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Discussion in the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+bicomplex"} variational bicomplex
            includes
          %ul
            %li#BarnichBrandt01
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Glenn+Barnich"}> Glenn Barnich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Friedemann+Brandt"} Friedemann Brandt
              = succeed "," do
                %em Covariant theory of asymptotic symmetries, conservation laws and central charges
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0111246"} arXiv:hep-th/0111246
          %p
            Discussion of a generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+groups"} discrete groups
            of symmetries includes
          %ul
            %li
              Anthony Ashton,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Conservation laws and non-Lie symmetries for linear PDEs
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/pdf/10.2991/jnmp.2008.15.3.5"} web
          %p
            The example of conserved currents in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            is discussed around (5.381) on p. 925 of
          %ul
            %li
              Vladimir Ivancevic, Tijana Ivancevi,
              %em Applied differential geometry: a modern introduction
          %p and also in
          %ul
            %li
              M. Francaviglia, M. Palese, E. Winterroth,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Locally variational invariant field equations and global currents: Chern-Simons theories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://cm.osu.cz/sites/default/files/contents/20-1/cm020-2012-1_13-22.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            and for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            in
          %ul
            %li
              G.Giachetta, L.Mangiarotti, G.Sardanashvily,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Noether conservation laws in higher-dimensional Chern-Simons theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0310067"} arXiv:math-ph/0310067
          %p
            A formalization of Noether’s theorem in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            is discussed in sections “2.7 Noether symmetries and equivariant structure” and “3.2 Local observables, conserved currents and higher Poisson bracket homotopy Lie algebras” of
          %ul
            %li#Schreiber13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Classical+field+theory+via+Cohesive+homotopy+types"} Classical field theory via Cohesive homotopy types
              (2013)
          %p
            A formalization of invariance of Lagrangians in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parametricity"} parametric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Atkey"}> Robert Atkey
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em From Parametricity to Conservation Laws, via Noether’s Theorem
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://popl.mpi-sws.org/2014/index.html"} Principles of Programming Languages (POPL) 2014
              (
              %a{:href => "http://bentnib.org/conservation-laws.pdf"}> pdf article
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://bentnib.org/posts/2014-01-29-popl-slides.html"} web slides
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://bentnib.org/docs/conservation-laws-20140124.pdf"} pdf slides
          %p
            Discussion in the convenient context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
          %ul
            %li#GiotopoulosSati23
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grigorios+Giotopoulos"}> Grigorios Giotopoulos
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
              %em
                Field Theory via Higher Geometry I:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Smooth+Sets+of+Fields"} Smooth Sets of Fields
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.16301"}> arXiv:2312.16301
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p On a generalization of symmetries valued in Lie groups for which the conservation equation becomes potentially nonabelian and does not give rise to symplectomorphisms:
          %ul
            %li
              Florian Girelli, Christopher Pollack, Aldo Riello.
              %em Beyond Noether: A Covariant Study of Poisson-Lie Symmetries in Low Dimensional Field Theory
              (2025). (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14942"}> arXiv:2505.14942
              ).
